    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The voltage across an inductor is given by $ u(t) = \\frac{d}{dt}[L(t)i(t)] $.  \nGiven $ L(t) = L_0(t + \\tanh t) $ and $ i(t) = \\cos(\\omega t) $, apply product rule:  \n$ u(t) = \\dot{L}(t)i(t) + L(t)\\dot{i}(t) $, where $ \\dot{L}(t) = L_0(1 + \\text{sech}^2 t) $, and $ \\dot{i}(t) = -\\omega \\sin(\\omega t) $.  \nThus, $ u(t) = L_0(1 + \\text{sech}^2 t)\\cos(\\omega t) - \\omega L_0(t + \\tanh t)\\sin(\\omega t) $, matching option C.\n\n$\\boxed{C}$",
