    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Food supplements, including trace minerals and vitamins are frequently advertised with promising health benefits. Which of the following substance could be consumed in excess, i.e. well above the recommended daily requirement?\n\n\nOptions: \nA. Vitamin C\nB. Vitamin D\nC. Zinc\nD. Vitamin A\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Vitamin A is fat-soluble and can accumulate in the liver, leading to toxicity (hypervitaminosis A) even at doses only moderately above the recommended daily intake\u2014symptoms include headache, dizziness, and liver damage. In contrast, vitamin C is water-soluble and excess is typically excreted in urine, while zinc and vitamin D, though potentially toxic in very high doses, have higher thresholds for acute toxicity compared to vitamin A. Vitamin A (especially preformed retinol from animal sources or supplements) is most notably associated with adverse effects from chronic excess intake.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
