    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5093",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The best available evidence for an association between amount of sugars and risk of dental caries comes from which type of study design?\n\n\nOptions: \nA. Case-control study\nB. Longitudinal study\nC. Non-randomised controlled\nD. Cross sectional observational\nE. Experimental study\nF. Meta-analysis\nG. Qualitative study\nH. Randomised controlled\nI. Cohort\nJ. Ecological study\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The best available evidence for an association between sugar intake and dental caries risk comes from studies that can establish temporal precedence and control for confounders, ideally through randomization. While ecological and cross-sectional studies have been historically used, the strongest evidence stems from prospective cohort studies and randomized controlled trials (RCTs), particularly those measuring sugar intake and caries outcomes over time. Among the options, cohort (I) and RCT (H) are strongest; however, given ethical constraints on assigning high-sugar diets, prospective cohort studies (e.g., the famous Darbyshire study) provide the most robust *observational* evidence consistently cited in systematic reviews.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
