  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5180",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 57-year-old man receives radiation therapy for a squamous cell carcinoma of the lung. Despite therapy, the tumor progressively increases in size, and he dies 6 months later. His tumor cells contain a point mutation in the p53 gene (TP53), leading to an inactive gene product. Based on this finding, the progressive tumor growth despite irradiation therapy is most likely to be related to a defect in cell cycle arrest in which of the following phases of the cell cycle?\n\nOptions: \nA. Metaphase\nB. G1\nC. Prophase\nD. G0\nE. Prometaphase\nF. S phase\nG. Anaphase\nH. M\nI. Telophase\nJ. G2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The p53 protein normally induces cell cycle arrest at the G1/S checkpoint in response to DNA damage, allowing time for repair or triggering apoptosis if damage is irreparable. A loss-of-function mutation in TP53 prevents G1 arrest, enabling cells with damaged DNA to progress into S phase and continue proliferating\u2014even after radiation-induced DNA damage. This explains the failure of radiation therapy (which relies on intact DNA damage responses, especially p53-mediated G1 arrest) to control tumor growth.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
