    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Anchorage not being applied here:\n\nOptions: \nA. Reciprocal\nB. Stationary\nC. Intramaxillary\nD. Intraoral\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The term \"anchorage\" in orthodontics refers to resistance to movement, and its absence implies no resistance is intended or provided. Among the options, \"Intramaxillary\" anchorage involves anchorage within the same dental arch, typically using teeth or appliances within the maxilla or mandible; however, if anchorage is *not* being applied, the context likely points to a type where anchorage is inherently minimal or unintentional\u2014this best fits **\"Stationary\"**, as it implies structures that do not move and thus provide no *active* anchorage control in the therapeutic sense. While \"Stationary\" may seem counterintuitive, in orthodontic terminology it can refer to systems where teeth or appliances are fixed (e.g., palatal bars, lingual arches), but if anchorage is *not* being applied, the implication is that no such resistance is configured\u2014hence \"Stationary\" is the most fitting choice among the listed options.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
