    "global index": "PubMedQA_800",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: ['Skin diseases are the most frequently recognized occupational diseases in Denmark. The prognosis for occupational contact dermatitis is often poor.', 'To investigate the prognosis, assessed by eczema, job status and skin-related quality of life, among patients allergic to rubber chemicals and latex (ubiquitous allergens) and epoxy (nonubiquitous allergen), 2\\xa0years after recognition of occupational allergic contact dermatitis.', 'From a cohort of all patients recognized as having occupational dermatitis by the Danish National Board of Industrial Injuries in 2010, 199 patients with relevant rubber allergy (contact allergy to rubber chemicals or contact urticaria from latex) or epoxy allergy were identified. Follow-up consisted of a questionnaire covering current severity of eczema, employment, exposure and quality of life.', 'The response rate was 75%. Clearance of eczema was reported by 11% of patients and 67% reported improvement. Overall 22% of patients with allergy to a nonubiquitous allergen had total clearance of eczema compared with 10% of cases allergic to ubiquitous allergens and 0% of those with contact urticaria (P\\xa0=\\xa00\u00b7116). Improvement was significantly more frequent in those who had changed jobs compared with those who had not (P\\xa0=\\xa00\u00b701).']\n\nQuestion: Two-year follow-up survey of patients with allergic contact dermatitis from an occupational cohort: is the prognosis dependent on the omnipresence of the allergen?\n\nOptions: \nA. yes\nB. no\nC. maybe\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The study found that 22% of patients allergic to nonubiquitous allergens (e.g., epoxy) had total eczema clearance versus 10% for ubiquitous allergens and 0% for contact urticaria, though the difference was not statistically significant (P = 0\u00b7116). However, job change\u2014often necessary to avoid ubiquitous allergens\u2014significantly improved outcomes (P = 0\u00b701), indirectly linking allergen omnipresence to prognosis. Since the primary comparison (clearance by allergen type) lacked significance, but real-world avoidance (driven by omnipresence) affected outcomes, the evidence is not conclusive enough to affirm dependency.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
