    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_312",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What are the core concepts of Schenkerian analysis theory \u2014 the fundamental structure (Ursatz) \u2014 comprised of?\n\nOptions: \nA. Fundamental tones (Diatonie) and arpeggios; Brechung (breaking, bass)\nB. Horizontal expansion (Ausfaltung) and Bassbrechung (bass breaking)\nC. Urlinie (fundamental line) and arpeggios; Brechung (breaking, bass)\nD. Urlinie (fundamental line) and Bassbrechung (bass breaking)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The core of Schenkerian analysis is the *Ursatz* (fundamental structure), consisting of two main elements: the *Urlinie* (fundamental line), a stepwise melodic descent\u2014often a third, fifth, or octave\u2014from scale degree 3, 5, or 8 down to 1, and the *Bassbrechung* (bass arpeggiation), where the bass moves from I to V to I (or similar arpeggiations). While *Brechung* and *Ausfaltung* appear in Schenker\u2019s writings, they are not part of the core *Ursatz*. Option D correctly identifies *Urlinie* and *Bassbrechung* as the foundational components.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
