    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Enharmonic relationships occur when two notes or keys sound the same but are spelled differently (e.g., C\u266f and D\u266d). Among the options, E minor and \u266fD minor both refer to the same pitch class set (E minor = E\u2013F\u266f\u2013G\u2013A\u2013B\u2013C\u2013D; \u266fD minor = D\u266f\u2013E\u266f\u2013F\u266f\u2013G\u266f\u2013A\u266f\u2013B\u2013C\u266f), which enharmonically equate to E minor (since D\u266f = E, E\u266f = F\u266f, G\u266f = A\u266d, A\u266f = B\u266d, C\u266f = D\u266d\u2014but standard spelling of E minor uses natural notes except F\u266f and C\u266f). However, strictly speaking, **E minor and \u266fD minor** are not standard key names\u2014\u266fD minor would be D\u266f minor, which *enharmonically equals* E minor (since D\u266f = E, though key signatures differ). This is the closest match.  \n\\boxed{B}",
