n, then C\u266f\u2013D is 1, D\u2013E\u266d is 2 \u2192 total 2? No: pitch names: \u266fC = D\u266d, so \u266fC = D\u266d. Then D\u266d to E\u266d is 2 semitones \u2192 major second. Wait: root is \u266dE. Augmented sixth interval above \u266dE is \u266fC (\u266dE\u2013G\u2013C\u2013\u266fD for German, but Italian is \u266dE\u2013G\u2013\u266fC). So \u266dE to \u266fC = 8 semitones. Inversion: \u266fC\u2013\u266dE ascending = 4 semitones? \u266fC\u2013D\u2013E\u266d = 2 semitones? \u266fC = D\u266d, so D\u266d\u2013E\u266d = 2 semitones = major second. So inverted interval is a major second. Option A says \u201cmajor second\u201d, D says \u201cmajor second\u201d too \u2014 wait A says \u201cIts inverted equivalent interval is a major second\u201d, D says \u201cThe inverted property is a major second\u201d. Both seem same. But check solfege: B says \u266fC\u2013\u266dE \u2014 that\u2019s the inverted interval (from top to bottom), yes, but solfege might be La\u2013Mi\u266d or something. However standard answer: augmented sixth invert to minor seventh? No. Let\u2019s recompute: augmented sixth = 8 semitones. Inversion = 12 \u2013 8 = 4 semitones (since circle of 12). 4 semitones = perfect fourth? No, 5 semitones = P4, 4 = major third? Wait: major third = 4 semitones (C\u2013E). Yes! So inverted interval is major third ascending. But earlier we got 3 semitones. Conflict arises from enharmonics. \u266dE to \u266fC: \u266dE = D\u266f, so D\u266f\u2013\u266fC = D\u266f\u2013C\u266f = descending major third, but ascending \u266fC\u2013D\u266f = major third (4 semitones). Yes! So \u266dE = D\u266f, so \u266dE\u2013\u266fC = D\u266f\u2013C\u266f, which ascending is C\u266f\u2013D\u266f = major second? No: C\u266f\u2013D\u266f is whole step = 2 semitones. Wait: D\u266f to C\u266f is descending major third (3 semitones down). Ascending C\u266f\u2013D\u266f = 2 semitones. This is messy. Standard fact: augmented sixth interval inverts to minor third. Because augmented sixth = 8 semitones; minor third = 3 semitones; 8 + 3 = 11 \u2260 12. Hmm. Actually interval inversion: size + inversion = 9 for simple intervals (e.g., m3 = 3, inversion = M6 = 6, 3+6=9). Augmented sixth is compound? No, sixth = 9 semitones for major sixth, augmented sixth = 10 semitones? Wait: major sixth = 9 semitones (C\u2013A). Augmented sixth = 10 semitones (C\u2013A\u266f). So augmented sixth = 10 semitones. Inversion = 12 \u2013 10 = 2 semitones = major second. Yes! So inverted interval is major second. Options A and D both say major second, but A says \u201cIts inverted equivalent interval is a major second\u201d \u2014 correct phrasing. D says \u201cThe inverted property is a major second\u201d \u2014 ambiguous. Likely A is best. So answer is A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
