    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_126",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which option matches the following scale?\n\nOptions: \nA. Phrygian mode with D as the II degree\nB. Phrygian mode with \u266fF as the VI degree\nC. Dorian mode with Si as the leading tone\nD. Locrian mode with Sol as the dominant tone\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let\u2019s analyze each option by identifying the scale implied and checking for consistency.\n\nA. Phrygian mode with D as the II degree: In Phrygian, the II degree is a whole step above the tonic, so if D is II, the tonic is C. Phrygian on C is C\u2013D\u266d\u2013E\u266d\u2013F\u2013G\u2013A\u266d\u2013B\u266d.\n\nB. Phrygian mode with \u266fF as the VI degree: In Phrygian, VI is a minor sixth above the tonic (e.g., on C: A\u266d). \u266fF would imply a raised sixth, inconsistent with Phrygian\u2019s flat sixth.\n\nC. Dorian mode with Si (B) as the leading tone: Leading tone is the seventh scale degree, which must be a half step below the tonic. So B as leading tone implies tonic = C. Dorian on C is C\u2013D\u2013E\u266d\u2013F\u2013G\u2013A\u2013B\u266d \u2014 but here B is natural, not B\u266d \u2014 contradiction.\n\nD. Locrian mode with Sol (G) as the dominant tone: Locrian has flattened second and fifth; dominant (V) is diminished. If G is V, then tonic is D\u266d (since G is a perfect fifth above D\u266d). Locrian on D\u266d is D\u266d\u2013E\u266d\u266d\u2013F\u266d\u2013G\u266d\u2013A\u266d\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u266d\u2013C\u266d. Here G is the raised version of G\u266d \u2014 wait, but \"Sol\" usually refers to G natural. In Locrian, the fifth is flattened, so the dominant should be G\u266d, not G. However, if we interpret \u201cSol\u201d as *scale degree* 5 (not pitch G), then yes \u2014 in any key, scale degree 5 is the dominant. Locrian\u2019s dominant is diminished, as expected.\n\nBut reconsider B more carefully: In Phrygian, \u266d6 is standard; if VI = \u266fF, then \u266d6 = \u266fF \u21d2 natural F, meaning the key has no flats/sharps? That would be E Phrygian (E\u2013F\u2013G\u2013A\u2013B\u2013C\u2013D) \u2014 yes! In E Phrygian, scale degrees: I=E, II=F, III=G, IV=A, V=B, **VI=\u266dC? Wait, no.** E Phrygian: E F G A B C D \u21d2 VI is C, not F. Let\u2019s compute: Phrygian interval pattern = 1 \u266d2 \u266d3 4 5 \u266d6 \u266d7. To have \u266fF as VI, VI = \u266fF \u21d2 \u266d6 = \u266fF \u21d2 6 = \u266d(\u266fF) = G? This is messy.\n\nWait\u2014maybe answer is **D**: In Locrian, the dominant (scale degree 5) is always a diminished fifth above the tonic (i.e., flatted), and it's still called the *dominant* functionally. So \u201cSol as the dominant tone\u201d means scale degree 5 = G, so tonic = D\u266d (since G is V of D\u266d), and Locrian on D\u266d fits.\n\nBut better: In Locrian on B (B Locrian: B C D E F G A), dominant = F (scale degree 5). If we want dominant = G, then scale must be A Locrian (A B C D E F G), dominant = E\u2014not G. Wait: What key has G as scale degree 5? C major, but Locrian on D\u266d has V = A\u266d. Hmm.\n\nActually, in **Locrian mode**, scale degree 5 is the *dominant*, regardless of pitch name. So \"Sol\" here may refer to *scale degree 5*, not the pitch G. Then D is plausible.\n\nBut check **C** again: Dorian with B as leading tone \u21d2 tonic = C. Dorian on C has B\u266d \u2014 but maybe they meant *leading tone* as the note just below tonic (B to C), so Dorian can\u2019t have natural B. So C is wrong.\n\nOption **A**: D as II in Phrygian \u2192 tonic = C\u266d? No\u2014Phrygian II is whole step above tonic, so II = D \u21d2 tonic = C. Phrygian C: C D\u266d E\u266d F G A\u266d B\u266d. Yes, that\u2019s valid.\n\nBut which one is *most likely* intended? Standard exam trick: Locrian\u2019s dominant (V) is diminished, and they often test that scale degree 5 is still called dominant.\n\nHowever, **D** says \u201cSol as the dominant tone\u201d\u2014if \u201cSol\u201d is pitch G, then tonic must be D (since G is perfect fifth above D). Locrian on D: D E\u266d F G A\u266d B\u266d C. Here G is indeed scale degree 5 (dominant), and it's a diminished fifth above D (yes\u2014D to G is perfect fourth, wait no: D to G is perfect fourth, but fifth would be A\u2014confusion).\n\nWait: Dominant is *fifth scale degree*, not necessarily a fifth above tonic pitch-wise. So in D Locrian: D (1) E\u266d (2) F (3) G (4) A\u266d (5)\u2026 So scale degree 5 = A\u266d, not G. So G is scale degree 4.\n\nTo get scale degree 5 = G, need tonic = C (G is 5th in C). But C Locrian: C D\u266d E\u266d F G A\u266d B\u266d \u21d2 scale degree 5 = G \u2014 yes! So C Locrian has G as dominant (scale degree 5). So D is correct if \u201cSol\u201d = G and tonic = C.\n\nYes! So D is correct.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
