    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_182",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If a Neapolitan sixth chord in F minor is used as a pivot chord to modulate to D flat major, what is the function label of the pivot chord in the new key?\n\nOptions: \nA. S_6\nB. S\u2161_6\nC. TS\u2165^6_4\nD. TSVI_6\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Neapolitan sixth chord in F minor is F\u266d\u2013A\u266d\u2013C\u266d (built on \u266dII), which functions as N\u2086. In F minor, this chord contains D\u266d and A\u266d, matching the tonic (D\u266d major) chord's notes D\u266d\u2013F\u2013A\u266d. In D\u266d major, the same chord (D\u266d\u2013F\u2013A\u266d) is the **submediant sixth chord**, i.e., TSVI\u2086 (since VI = A\u266d, but here the root is D\u266d, so it\u2019s actually the tonic triad in first inversion\u2014wait: D\u266d\u2013F\u2013A\u266d is I in D\u266d major; in first inversion it\u2019s I\u2076, not VI). Correction: The Neapolitan in F minor is E\u266e\u2013G\u2013C (enharmonically F\u266d\u2013A\u266d\u2013C\u266d), but *standard notation*: Neapolitan in F minor is \u266dII = G\u266d major chord: G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d. This chord functions in D\u266d major as the **supertonic chord in second inversion**? No\u2014G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d is IV in D\u266d major, and in first inversion (B\u266d\u2013D\u266d\u2013G\u266d) is IV\u2086. But Neapolitan is *always* built on \u266dII and is a major triad. In F minor, \u266dII = G\u266d major = G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d. In D\u266d major, that same chord (G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d) is **IV**, but the question says *pivot chord*, implying it serves two functions. G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d = IV in D\u266d major, but none of the options say IV\u2086. Let\u2019s re-express: D\u266d major keys: I = D\u266d, VI = B\u266d, II = E\u266d, \u266dII = G\u266d. So G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d is indeed \u266dII\u2013IV\u2013I \u2014 wait, no: \u266dII in D\u266d major is G\u266d, so \u266dII chord = G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d = **IV chord**, since D\u266d major\u2019s fourth degree is G\u266d. So it\u2019s IV, not VI. But option C is TS\u2165^6_4 \u2014 perhaps misnotation. Standard Roman numeral for Neapolitan in D\u266d major is N\u2086 = \u266dII\u2086 = G\u266d\u2086, i.e., G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d in first inversion. So its function is N\u2086, but options don\u2019t have N. However, in D\u266d major, the same chord (G\u266d\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d) is *also* the **subdominant sixth chord**, i.e., S\u2086 (since IV is subdominant). So S\u2086 = IV\u2086. Option A is S\u2086. Yes. Thus, answer is A.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
