    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_211",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which harmonic major or minor mode could the following diminished triad possibly belong to?\n\nOptions: \nA. \u266dg harmonic minor\nB. \u266dG harmonic major\nC. \u266dE harmonic major and \u266de harmonic minor\nD. All of the above\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A diminished triad (e.g., G\u00b0 = G\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d) can appear in several harmonic modes due to the presence of the lowered sixth or seventh degrees. In harmonic minor, the leading tone (raised seventh) creates a diminished chord on scale degree vii\u00b0, e.g., in g minor: F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C \u2192 F\u266f\u00b0, but G\u00b0 appears in **\u266dG harmonic major** (scale: \u266dG\u2013\u266dA\u2013\u266dB\u266d\u2013\u266dC\u2013\u266dD\u2013\u266dE\u2013F\u2013\u266dG), where vii\u00b0 is G\u00b0. In **\u266de harmonic minor** (scale: \u266dE\u2013F\u2013G\u266d\u2013\u266dA\u2013\u266dB\u2013C\u266d\u2013D\u2013\u266dE), vii\u00b0 is D\u00b0, not G\u00b0. However, G\u00b0 can also arise in **\u266dG harmonic major**, and G\u00b0 is the vii\u00b0 chord in **e harmonic minor** (E minor: F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C\u266f \u2192 F\u266f\u00b0, but wait\u2014G\u00b0 isn\u2019t there). Rechecking: In **\u266dG harmonic major**, notes are \u266dG, \u266dA, \u266dB\u266d, \u266dC, \u266dD, \u266dE, **F**, \u266dG; vii\u00b0 = **G\u00b0** (G\u2013B\u266d\u2013D\u266d) \u2014 yes! In **\u266dg harmonic minor**, notes: \u266dG, \u266dA, \u266dB\u266d, \u266dC, \u266dD, \u266dE\u266d, F, \u266dG; vii\u00b0 = **F\u00b0** (F\u2013A\u266d\u2013C\u266d), not G\u00b0. But G\u00b0 appears as iii\u00b0 in \u266dE harmonic major? \u266dE major: \u266dE\u2013F\u2013G\u2013\u266dA\u266d\u2013\u266dB\u266d\u2013\u266dC\u2013D\u2013\u266dE; iii\u00b0 = G\u00b0 \u2014 yes! And in \u266de harmonic minor: \u266dE\u2013F\u2013G\u266d\u2013\u266dA\u266d\u2013\u266dB\u266d\u2013C\u266d\u2013D\u2013\u266dE; vii\u00b0 = D\u00b0, but G\u00b0? G natural is \u266d3 in \u266de minor \u2014 not standard. However, option C says \u201c\u266dE harmonic major and \u266de harmonic minor\u201d \u2014 G\u00b0 is indeed iii\u00b0 in \u266dE harmonic major and could appear chromatically elsewhere. Since **all listed modes contain G\u00b0 somewhere**, D is plausible. But strict diatonic occurrence: G\u00b0 is vii\u00b0 in **\u266dG harmonic major** and iii\u00b0 in **\u266dE harmonic major** \u2014 so A (\u266dg hm) doesn\u2019t yield G\u00b0 diatonically. Yet the question says *\u201cpossibly belong to\u201d* \u2014 so any mode where G\u00b0 is used (even non-diatonically) qualifies \u2014 then D is correct.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
