    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In standard music notation, pitch ranges are categorized as follows:  \n- Low pitch range includes the great octave (capital letters G, F, E, D, C) and small octave (lowercase c, d, e), but **not** capital letter groups beyond C (e.g., C, D, E in uppercase typically refer to lower registers in some systems, but \"capital letter group\" usually means C\u2081\u2013B\u2081, i.e., great octave).  \nOption A incorrectly includes the *unaccented octave* (\u901a\u5e38\u6307\u5c0f\u5b57\u4e00\u7ec4, i.e., small letter group) alongside capital and small letter one groups as middle pitch\u2014actually, unaccented octave (c\u2081\u2013b\u2081) is part of the middle range, and small letter one group is the same; however, the capital letter group (C\u2082\u2013B\u2082?) is ambiguous, but traditionally capital letters denote lower octaves.  \nBut critically, **Option B** is incorrect because it lumps small letter group (c\u2013b), small letter one group (c\u2081\u2013b\u2081), and small letter two group (c\u2082\u2013b\u2082) into the *middle* pitch range\u2014yet small letter two group is generally considered *high* range.  \nThus, **B is the most clearly incorrect statement**.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
