    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In which key is the correct resolution from mi-la to re-la?\n\nOptions: \nA. A major\nB. G major\nC. C major\nD. D major\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The progression from mi\u2013la to re\u2013la implies moving from scale degrees 3\u20136 to 2\u20136. In solf\u00e8ge, \"mi\" = E, \"la\" = A; \"re\" = D, \"la\" = A. So the chord progression is E\u2013A (E major triad?) to D\u2013A (D major triad?). However, more plausibly, it refers to melodic motion: ascending minor third (mi to la is m3), resolving down to re\u2013la (a fifth below). But \u201cresolution\u201d suggests harmonic function\u2014perhaps dominant to tonic? In A major, mi = C#, la = A, re = B; so C#\u2013A to B\u2013A isn\u2019t standard. In C major: mi = E, la = A, re = D \u2192 E\u2013A (E major) to D\u2013A (D major); E major is V in A minor but not diatonic to C major. Better: in **D major**, scale: D E F# G A B C#. mi = F#, la = B, re = E. So F#\u2013B to E\u2013B: F#\u2013B\u2013D# (F# major = V of B major) to E\u2013B\u2013G (E major = IV of A major)? Hmm.\n\nWait\u2014interpret \u201cmi\u2013la\u201d as a *chord* where mi is bass? Or melody? Perhaps \u201cmi\u2013la\u201d is the interval C\u2013F (if in C major, mi=C? No\u2014solfege fixed: do=tonic. So in **G major**: do=G, re=A, mi=B, la=D. So mi\u2013la = B\u2013D (minor third); re\u2013la = A\u2013D (perfect fourth\u2014not a typical resolution). In **C major**: mi=E, la=A (E\u2013A = M3), re=D, la=A (D\u2013A = P5). E\u2013A is V of Am; D\u2013A is IV\u2013tonic in A? But not standard.\n\nWait! The *descending* fifth (dominant to tonic): from mi\u2013la to re\u2013la\u2014if la stays same (A), then mi\u2192re descending step? That\u2019s like E\u2192D (in A major, mi=C#? no). In **A major**: do=A, mi=C#, la=F#; so mi\u2013la=C#\u2013F# (M3); re=B, la=F# \u21d2 B\u2013F# (d5) \u2014 dissonant, resolves to... not matching.\n\nBut the *only* diatonic case where 3\u20136 moves to 2\u20136 as a resolution is in **C major**: chord vi (Am) to V (G)? No.\n\nRe-evaluate: In **D major**, do=D, mi=F#, la=B. So mi\u2013la=F#\u2013B (M3). re=E, la=B \u21d2 E\u2013B (P5). F#\u2013B (V of B minor) resolving to E\u2013B (IV of A)? Not ideal.\n\nAh: Perhaps \u201cmi\u2013la\u201d = major third up (e.g., E\u2013G#), resolving to \u201cre\u2013la\u201d = perfect fourth (D\u2013G)? No.\n\nStandard resolution: dominant (V) to tonic (I). In **G major**, V = D, I = G. mi = B, la = D \u2192 B\u2013D (m3); re = A, la = D \u2192 A\u2013D (P4). B\u2013D is v (minor), not V.\n\nWait\u2014if we consider *melodic* resolution of leading tone: ti\u2192do. mi\u2013la to re\u2013la may mean lowering the upper note? Hmm.\n\nActually: In **C major**, scale: C D E F G A B. mi=E, la=A (E\u2013A = M3); re=D, la=A (D\u2013A = P5). E\u2013A is V of Am, D\u2013A is IV of A, not resolution.\n\nBut if la is *fixed* and mi\u2192re descending whole step, that\u2019s like V\u2013I if la=tonic? In **A major**, la=A? No, la is 6th. So la \u2260 tonic.\n\nThe *only* key where mi and re are adjacent diatonically is C major (E and D are adjacent), and A\u2013A? Wait\u2014re\u2013la = D\u2013A (P5), mi\u2013la = E\u2013A (M3). The M3 resolves to P5? Not typical.\n\nActually, E\u2013A to D\u2013A is a descending chromatic motion in the upper voice? E\u2192D is stepwise down\u2014common voice-leading. This works diatonically in **C major** (E and D are diatonic notes). A remains constant. So **C major** allows smooth resolution.\n\nThus, the answer is **C. C major**.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
