    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_334",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In the F Jue Ya-yue scale, which of the following correctly describes the relationship between Bian Gong and Bian Zhi?\n\nOptions: \nA. The single note formed by the Bian Zhi \u266d\u266dA and the Bian Gong C is an augmented third\nB. The compound notes formed by the Bian Zhi G and the Bian Gong C is a perfect eleventh\nC. The compound notes formed by the Bian Zhi \u266dG and the Bian Gong \u266dC is a perfect eleventh\nD. The compound interval formed by the Bian Zhi G and the Bian Gong \u266dC is a diminished eleventh\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In the F Jue Ya-yue scale (pentatonic), Bian Gong is C and Bian Zhi is G (natural), as derived from the modal shift in Chinese traditional theory. A compound interval spans more than one octave; a perfect eleventh is the compound form of a perfect fourth (5 semitones + 12 = 17 semitones). From G to C is a perfect fourth (5 semitones), so G to C in the next octave is a perfect eleventh (17 semitones). Option B correctly states this relationship using natural G and C.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
