    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_336",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The interval E-G can be found in which of the following tonalities?\n\nOptions: \nA. B Pentatonic yue mode\nB. E Ya-yue Jiao mode\nC. G Qing-yue Shang mode\nD. Yan-yue Zhi mode\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The interval E\u2013G is a minor third (3 semitones). In Chinese modal systems, the Ya-yue scale has specific mode structures: in Jiao mode (based on E), the scale is E\u2013F\u266f\u2013G\u266f\u2013A\u2013B\u2013C\u266f\u2013D\u266f\u2013E, but E\u2013G is not diatonically present. In Qing-yue Shang mode (based on G), the scale is G\u2013A\u2013B\u266d\u2013C\u2013D\u2013E\u2013F\u2013G, so E\u2013G is a descending major sixth or ascending minor third (G to E is minor sixth; E to G is minor third), and E is the 6th scale degree, G is the tonic \u2014 yes, E\u2013G occurs as a stepwise minor third within the mode. Yan-yue Zhi mode (based on D\u266f/E\u266d) does not naturally contain E\u2013G as a diatonic interval. Thus, option C is correct.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
