shed second (since diatonically D\u2192C is minor second, sharpening D makes it diminished). In E harmonic minor, C and D\u266f are both present, and D\u266f to C is a diminished second interval \u2014 possible in E harmonic minor.\n\nThus option B (E harmonic major) is wrong \u2014 harmonic major has \u266d6, so E harmonic major: E, F\u266f, G\u266f, A, B, C\u266e, D\u266f \u2014 same as harmonic minor! Wait: harmonic major is \u266d6, not \u266d3. So E harmonic major: E, F\u266f, G\u266f, A, B, C\u266e, D\u266f \u2014 yes, same pitch set as E harmonic minor? No: harmonic minor is 1 2 \u266d3 4 5 \u266d6 \u266d7 \u2192 E, F\u266f, G\u266e, A, B, C\u266e, D\u266f. Ah! Critical: harmonic minor has \u266d3 (G\u266e), while harmonic major has \u266d6 (C\u266e) but natural minor third (G\u266e). So E harmonic major: 1 2 3 4 5 \u266d6 7 \u2192 E, F\u266f, G\u266f, A, B, C\u266e, D\u266f. Yes \u2014 so E harmonic major has G\u266f (not G\u266e), and C\u266e (\u266d6), D\u266f (7). So D\u266f and C\u266e are present, adjacent in scale: ..., C\u266e, D\u266f. So ascending C\u266e\u2192D\u266f is augmented second; descending D\u266f\u2192C\u266e is diminished second (same as \u266fd\u2081\u2013c\u2082 if d\u2081 = D\u266e, \u266fd\u2081 = D\u266f, c\u2082 = C\u266e).\n\nTherefore, **B. E harmonic major** contains both D\u266f and C\u266e in the scale, allowing this diminished-second interval.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
