    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In G major natural scale, the interval that can resolve to B-D is\n\nOptions: \nA. #la-fa\nB. si-mi\nC. la-#fa\nD. la-#mi\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In G major, B-D forms a minor third (B to D is 3 semitones). To resolve to B-D, we need a chord whose root leads smoothly (typically by step or tritone resolution) and includes notes that resolve to B and D. Option A (#la\u2013fa) = #A\u2013F = A\u266f\u2013F, which is not diatonic and doesn\u2019t resolve well to B\u2013D. Option B (si\u2013mi) = B\u2013E, and the dominant seventh chord in G major is D\u2013F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C, but B\u2013E is part of E minor or dominant function (V of Am), not directly resolving to B\u2013D. Option C (la\u2013#fa) = A\u2013F\u266f \u2014 this is the triad A\u2013C\u266f\u2013E, i.e., A major, whose root A resolves up to B, and F\u266f resolves up to G (but not directly to D). However, the leading-tone chord in G major is F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C (vii\u00b0), where F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C resolves to G\u2013B\u2013D. Wait\u2014B\u2013D is the 3rd and 5th of G major triad, so resolution typically comes from viio\u2077 (F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C\u2013E\u266d) \u2192 I (G\u2013B\u2013D). But among options, only A. #la\u2013fa = A\u266f\u2013F: this interval is a diminished fifth (tritone), and A\u266f\u2013F resolves outward to B\u2013G (or inward to G\u266f\u2013F\u266f), not B\u2013D. Re-evaluate: B\u2013D is the 3rd and 5th of G; the dominant chord is D\u2013F\u266f\u2013A, so the 3rd\u20135th interval is F\u266f\u2013A, not resolving *to* B\u2013D. Actually, the question may mean which interval resolves *to* B\u2013D as its final sonority. The tritone F\u2013B resolves to E\u2013C\u266f or G\u2013B. Wait\u2014maybe they mean which pair of notes, when moved, becomes B\u2013D. Option C: la\u2013#fa = A\u2013F\u266f. A moves down to G, F\u266f moves up to G? That gives G\u2013G, no. Hmm. Another view: in G major, scale degrees: G(1), A(2), B(3), C(4), D(5), E(6), F\u266f(7). So \u201csi\u201d=B, \u201cmi\u201d=D \u2014 but si\u2013mi = B\u2013D is already the interval itself! But the question says \u201ccan resolve to B\u2013D\u201d, implying the interval is not B\u2013D. Wait\u2014maybe solf\u00e8ge is fixed do: in G major, tonic do = G, so re= A, mi= B, sol= D, si= F\u266f? No\u2014fixed do: C=do, so G=so, A=la, B=ti, C=do, D=re, E=mi, F\u266f=fi. So in fixed do, B=ti, D=re. Then B\u2013D = ti\u2013re = minor third (3 semitones). Option B: si\u2013mi = ti\u2013mi = B\u2013E. B to E is augmented fourth (tritone), which resolves to C\u2013D or A\u2013B? Not directly to B\u2013D. Option A: #la\u2013fa = #A\u2013F = A\u266f\u2013F (tritone), resolves outward to G\u2013G\u266f or inward to A\u2013F\u266f \u2014 but A\u2013F\u266f is the dominant chord\u2019s 3rd\u20135th. A\u2013F\u266f resolves to G\u2013E? No. Let's consider chord resolution: viio\u2077 in G is F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C\u2013E\u266d \u2192 G\u2013B\u2013D. So intervals in viio\u2077: F\u266f\u2013A (M3), A\u2013C (m3), C\u2013E\u266d (m3), etc. Among options, none match exactly. But option C: la\u2013#fa = A\u2013F\u266f = inverted to F\u266f\u2013A, which is in viio chord. So F\u266f\u2013A resolves up/down to G\u2013B \u2014 and B is in B\u2013D. So possibly A\u2013F\u266f resolves such that A\u2192B and F\u266f\u2192D? A up to B (step), F\u266f up to G? Not D. Wait\u2014F\u266f\u2192G, A\u2192B gives G\u2013B, then add D? Maybe it's about voice leading: in V\u2077 (D\u2013F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C), F\u266f\u2192G, A\u2192G or B, C\u2192B, D\u2192E or C. So A can move to B (then with D unchanged gives B\u2013D). Yes! In D\u2013F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C \u2192 G\u2013B\u2013D, A\u2192B, F\u266f\u2192G, C\u2192B, D stays. So the interval A\u2013C (la\u2013fa in fixed do? la=A, fa=C) but fa is C, and la\u2013fa = A\u2013C (minor third), which resolves to B\u2013B? Not right. Option C is la\u2013#fa = A\u2013F\u266f. In the dominant chord D\u2013F\u266f\u2013A\u2013C, F\u266f\u2013A is part of it, and F\u266f\u2013A resolves to G\u2013B (F\u266f\u2192G, A\u2192B), giving G\u2013B, and D remains \u2192 G\u2013B\u2013D. So if we consider only F\u266f\u2013A (la\u2013#fa), it resolves to G\u2013B, and with D added (from root), we get G\u2013B\u2013D, containing B\u2013D. Since B\u2013D is the top two notes of G major triad, and F\u266f\u2013A resolves to G\u2013B, overlapping B is shared. So option C is best.  \n\\boxed{C}",
