    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Compared the Ya-yue Shang mode with \u266dB as the Zhi note to that with \u266dB as the tonic, the number of key signatures in the former and the latter is:\n\nOptions: \nA. the same\nB. the former has two fewer flat signs than the latter\nC. the latter has one more flat sign than the former\nD. the former has one less sharp sign than the latter\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In Chinese traditional music theory, the Ya-yue Shang mode uses \u266dB as the Zhi (dominant), implying a key center derived from the fifth degree of the scale. When \u266dB is the Zhi, the tonic is E (since B\u266d is the fifth of E\u266d, but in Ya-yue, Shang mode typically centers on D with B\u266d as Zhi, yielding D Dorian: D E F G A B\u266d C D \u2192 1 flat). If \u266dB were the tonic (i.e., B\u266d major or B\u266d Dorian), it would have 2 flats (B\u266d, E\u266d). Thus, the former (Zhi = \u266dB) has 1 flat (B\u266d), the latter (tonic = \u266dB) has 2 flats. So the former has one fewer flat than the latter \u2014 option C matches: \u201cthe latter has one more flat sign than the former.\u201d\n\n\\boxed{C}",
