    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In traditional Chinese modal theory, the tonic (Gong) anchors the mode; modes sharing the same tonic belong to the same \u201ckey.\u201d  \nOption A: \u266dE Ya-yue Shang \u2192 tonic is \u266dE.  \nOption B: Qing-yue Yu with \u266dG as Gong \u2192 tonic is \u266dG.  \nOption C: Yan-yue Zhi with \u266dG as Run (i.e., fifth degree), so Gong = \u266dC (since Run = G solf\u00e8ge = \u266dG implies Gong = \u266dC), but Yan-yue uses different pitch standards\u2014still, tonic is \u266dC.  \nOption D: Pentatonic with G as Jue (third degree), so tonic (Gong) is E\u266d (since Jue = 3rd, and in Chinese system, E\u266d\u2013F\u2013G\u2013A\u2013C \u21d2 Gong = E\u266d). Thus D also has tonic \u266dE. So A and D share tonic \u266dE; B and C differ. Therefore, **B** and **C** are not on the same tonic as A/D, but the question asks for the *one* that is *not* related by the same tonic\u2014likely meaning the outlier among options sharing a common tonic. However, only A and D share \u266dE; B and C each have different tonics (\u266dG and \u266dC). Since the question expects one answer, and **C** uses Yan-yue (Tang-style), where Run = \u266dG implies a different scale structure (e.g., \u266dC\u2013\u266dD\u2013\u266dE\u2013F\u2013\u266dG), tonic is \u266dC, unlike A/D\u2019s \u266dE and B\u2019s \u266dG. Yet **C** is most distinct due to Yan-yue\u2019s unique tuning and non-Heptatonic alignment\u2014thus **C** is least related.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
