      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{A}\n\nJohn C. Calhoun's argument in the \"South Carolina Exposition and Protest\" centers on states' rights to judge constitutional violations by the federal government, echoing the Democratic-Republican response to the Alien and Sedition Acts in the Kentucky and Virginia Resolutions. Those resolutions, authored by Jefferson and Madison, similarly asserted state authority to nullify unconstitutional federal laws. This parallel makes option A the most historically consistent choice.",
