    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The ethics of conducting archaeological research on sites with human remains of indigenous North American ancestors is addressed by the Native American Graves Protection and Repatriation Act. Problems often arise when:\n\nOptions: \nA. neither archaeologists nor Native Americans can agree on the exact location of burials at the site.\nB. burials are so ancient that establishing historical or genealogical connections with modern, living groups of people is impossible.\nC. archaeologists are not culturally sensitive to the spiritual beliefs of the Native Americans.\nD. there is a lack of funding to properly conduct archaeological research.\nE. there are no Native Americans who have been trained as archaeologists and no archaeologists who are Native American.\nF. both non-indigenous archaeologists and Native Americans are allowed onto the site at the same time.\nG. Native Americans and archaeologists have different interpretations of the artifacts found.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Problems often arise when burials are so ancient that establishing historical or genealogical connections with modern, living groups of people is impossible, as this complicates compliance with NAGPRA's requirement for cultural affiliation. This challenge undermines efforts to repatriate remains and artifacts to rightful descendant communities. Such scientific and ethical ambiguities create tension between archaeological practice and indigenous rights.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
