i, when nums[i] is the maximum:\n    # - If we take only nums[i]: min = nums[i]\n    # - If we take nums[i] and some subset S of {nums[0], ..., nums[i-1]}: min = min(S \u222a {nums[i]})\n    # Since the array is sorted, nums[i] \u2265 all previous elements, so min = min(S) if S is non-empty, else nums[i]\n    \n    # So the sum of minimums for subsets with max = nums[i] is:\n    # nums[i] (for singleton) + (sum of min(S) for all non-empty S \u2286 {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}) + nums[i] * (2^i - 1)\n    # where the last term accounts for subsets where we take nums[i] and a non-empty S, but the min is from S\n    \n    # Let's define:\n    # sum_min[i] = sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of nums[0..i]\n    # Then sum_min[i] = sum_min[i-1] + (sum of minimums of subsets that include nums[i])\n    # And sum of minimums of subsets that include nums[i] = nums[i] + sum_min[i-1] + nums[i] * (2^(i) - 1)? No.\n    \n    # Correct recurrence:\n    # Consider subsets of {nums[0], ..., nums[i]}. They either don't contain nums[i] (sum_min[i-1]) or do contain nums[i].\n    # For subsets that contain nums[i]: for each subset T of {nums[0], ..., nums[i-1]}, we have subset T \u222a {nums[i]}.\n    # The minimum of T \u222a {nums[i]} is min(min(T), nums[i]) = min(T) since nums[i] is largest.\n    # So if T is empty, min = nums[i]. If T is non-empty, min = min(T).\n    # There are 2^i subsets T (including empty). So sum of mins for subsets containing nums[i] = nums[i] + sum_min[i-1].\n    # Therefore: sum_min[i] = sum_min[i-1] + nums[i] + sum_min[i-1] = 2 * sum_min[i-1] + nums[i]\n    \n    # But we want, for each i, the sum of minimums of subsets of {nums[0], ..., nums[i-1]} (not including nums[i]).\n    # Let prefix_sum[i] = sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of nums[0..i-1]\n    # Then prefix_sum[0] = 0, prefix_sum[1] = nums[0], etc.\n    \n    # Actually, let's define prefix_sum[i] as the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of the first i elements (indices 0..i-1).\n    # Then prefix_sum[i] = 2 * prefix_sum[i-1] + nums[i-1]\n    \n    # For our problem, when nums[i] is the maximum, the sum of minimums over all subsets with max=nums[i] is:\n    # prefix_sum[i] + nums[i] * (2^i) ??? Let's verify with small example.\n    \n    # Simpler approach found in known solutions:\n    # Sort the array. For each i, the contribution is nums[i]^2 * (prefix_sum up to i-1 + nums[i])\n    # where prefix_sum up to i-1 is the sum of minimums of all subsets of elements before i.\n    \n    # Known solution pattern:\n    # After sorting, for each i:\n    #   contribution = nums[i]^2 * (1 + 2 * (sum of min for subsets ending before i))\n    # Actually, the standard solution is:\n    #   ans += nums[i] * nums[i] * (prefix + nums[i])\n    #   prefix = 2 * prefix + nums[i]\n    \n    # Let's trace with [2,1,4] sorted -> [1,2,4]\n    # i=0: nums[0]=1\n    #   contribution = 1^2 * (0 + 1) = 1\n    #   prefix = 2*0 + 1 = 1\n    # i=1: nums[1]=2\n    #   contribution = 2^2 * (1 + 2) = 4 * 3 = 12\n    #   prefix = 2*1 + 2 = 4\n    # i=2: nums[2]=4\n    #   contribution = 4^2 * (4 + 4) = 16 * 8 = 128\n    # Total = 1 + 12 + 128 = 141 \u2713\n    \n    # So the algorithm is:\n    # Sort nums\n    # prefix = 0\n    # for i in range(n):\n    #   result = (result + nums[i] * nums[i] * (prefix + nums[i])) % MOD\n    #   prefix = (2 * prefix + nums[i]) % MOD\n    \n    nums.sort()\n    prefix = 0\n    result = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        result = (result + nums[i] * nums[i] * (prefix + nums[i])) % MOD\n        prefix = (2 * prefix + nums[i]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
