tput 1\n\n3\r\n1 1 1 0 0 1 0 1\r\n\nThe sample output corresponds to the following actions:\n\n- Find potions of types 2,3,1 in this order. Pick up all of them.\n- Find potions of types 3,2 in this order. Do not pick up any of them.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Do not pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-2 monster. Use one type-2 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-1 monster. Use one type-1 potion to defeat it.\n\nIn this sequence of actions, the value of K is 3.\nThere is no way to avoid defeat with K\\leq 2, so the sought value of K _ {{\\min}} is 3.\r\nThere are multiple sequences of actions that satisfy K=3 and allow him to avoid defeat; you may print any of them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4\r\n2 3\r\n1 4\r\n2 1\r\n1 2\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe will inevitably be defeated by the first monster he encounters.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30\r\n1 25\r\n1 2\r\n1 10\r\n1 18\r\n2 18\r\n1 11\r\n2 11\r\n1 21\r\n1 6\r\n2 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 11\r\n1 24\r\n1 11\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n1 18\r\n2 25\r\n1 8\r\n1 10\r\n1 11\r\n2 18\r\n2 10\r\n1 10\r\n2 2\r\n1 24\r\n1 10\r\n2 10\r\n1 25\r\n2 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\r\n1 1 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    events = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        t = int(data[index])\n        x = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        events.append((t, x))\n    \n    # Count required potions for each monster type\n    monster_count = defaultdict(int)\n    potion_events = []  # Store indices of potion events\n    monster_events = []  # Store (type, index) of monster events\n    \n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            potion_events.append(i)\n        else:  # t == 2\n            monster_count[x] += 1\n            monster_events.append((x, i))\n    \n    # Check if we have enough potions for each monster type\n    potion_available = defaultdict(int)\n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            potion_available[x] += 1\n    \n    for monster_type, count in monster_count.items():\n        if potion_available[monster_type] < count:\n            print(-1)\n            return\n    \n    # Greedy strategy: when encountering a monster, use the earliest available potion of that type\n    # We want to minimize the maximum number of potions held at any time.\n    # We'll simulate and decide which potions to pick up optimally.\n    \n    # We'll use a greedy approach: for each monster, we must have a potion of its type.\n    # We'll try to delay picking up potions until necessary.\n    # We maintain a list of available potions (by type) that we have picked up.\n    # For each monster event, we need to use a potion of the correct type.\n    # We want to minimize peak inventory.\n    \n    # We'll simulate backwards: for each monster, we need to reserve one potion.\n    # We'll assign the latest possible potion event for each monster to minimize early holding.\n    # Then we'll determine which potions to pick up: only those assigned to monsters or needed to cover constraints.\n    \n    # Step 1: For each monster type, collect all potion event indices and monster event indices.\n    potion_indices_by_type = defaultdict(list)\n    monster_indices_by_type = defaultdict(list)\n    \n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            potion_indices_by_type[x].append(i)\n        else:\n            monster_indices_by_type[x].append(i)\n    \n    # For each monster type, we need to assign one potion to each monster.\n    # We want to assign the latest possible potion (to minimize early holding).\n    # But we must ensure that the potion comes before the monster.\n    # So for each monster at position m, we assign the latest potion event before m of that type.\n    \n    assigned_potions = defaultdict(list)  # type -> list of potion event indices assigned to monsters\n    used_potion_events = set()\n    \n    for monster_type in monster_indices_by_type:\n        monster_positions = sorted(monster_indices_by_type[monster_type])\n        potion_positions = sorted(potion_indices_by_type[monster_type])\n        \n        # For each monster, assign the latest available potion that comes before it and hasn't been used\n        # We traverse monsters in order and assign the latest possible potion that is before the monster and not used.\n        # To minimize peak, we want to use as late as possible.\n        available = potion_positions[:]\n        for monster_pos in monster_positions:\n            # Find the latest potion index < monster_pos that is still available\n            candidate = -1\n            for i, p in enumerate(available):\n                if p < monster_pos:\n                    candidate = p\n                else:\n                    break\n            if candidate == -1:\n                print(-1)\n                return\n            assigned_potions[monster_type].append(candidate)\n            available.remove(candidate)\n            used_potion_events.add(candidate)\n    \n    # Now we know which potion events must be picked up: exactly the ones assigned to monsters.\n    # But we might have extra potions. We can choose to pick them up or not.\n    # However, we want to minimize the maximum number of potions held at any time.\n    # We can simulate the entire process and decide for each potion event whether to pick it up.\n    # We know that the assigned ones must be picked up.\n    # For unassigned potion events, we can choose to skip them unless necessary to avoid exceeding capacity? \n    # Actually, since we've already assigned the minimal set needed (each monster gets one), we can skip all others.\n    # Why? Because if we pick up an extra potion, it increases the inventory without helping any monster.\n    # So optimal strategy: pick up ONLY the potions that are assigned to monsters.\n    \n    # But wait: what if we have two monsters of same type, and we have three potions?\n    # We assigned the last two potions to the two monsters. The first potion is extra.\n    # We can skip the first one. That minimizes peak.\n    \n    # So: for each potion event, we pick it up if and only if it was assigned to a monster.\n    action_list = []\n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            if i in used_potion_events:\n                action_list.append(1)\n            else:\n                action_list.append(0)\n    \n    # Now compute the maximum inventory during the journey.\n    inventory = defaultdict(int)\n    max_inventory = 0\n    for i, (t, x) in enumerate(events):\n        if t == 1:\n            if i in used_potion_events:\n                inventory[x] += 1\n                max_inventory = max(max_inventory, sum(inventory.values()))\n        else:  # t == 2\n            # Must have at least one potion of type x\n            inventory[x] -= 1\n            if inventory[x] < 0:\n                # This shouldn't happen because we assigned exactly the right amount\n                print(-1)\n                return\n    \n    print(max_inventory)\n    print(\" \".join(map(str, action_list)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
