      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef count_sub_multisets(nums, l, r):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Count frequency of each number\n    freq = {}\n    for num in nums:\n        freq[num] = freq.get(num, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Sort unique numbers to process in order\n    unique_nums = sorted(freq.keys())\n    \n    # dp[i][s] represents number of ways to get sum s using numbers up to index i\n    # But we can optimize space using 1D DP\n    max_sum = r\n    \n    # Initialize dp: only sum 0 possible initially (empty multiset)\n    dp = [0] * (max_sum + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # empty multiset\n    \n    for num in unique_nums:\n        count = freq[num]\n        if num == 0:\n            # For zeros: each zero doesn't change sum but doubles the count of subsets\n            dp = [(x * (count + 1)) % MOD for x in dp]\n            continue\n            \n        # Create new_dp as copy of current dp (not taking any of current num)\n        new_dp = dp[:]\n        \n        # For each occurrence of current num (from 1 to count)\n        for k in range(1, count + 1):\n            current_val = k * num\n            if current_val > max_sum:\n                break\n            # Add contributions: taking k copies of num\n            for s in range(max_sum - current_val, -1, -1):\n                if dp[s] > 0:\n                    new_dp[s + current_val] = (new_dp[s + current_val] + dp[s]) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n    \n    # Sum counts for sums in [l, r]\n    result = 0\n    for s in range(l, r + 1):\n        result = (result + dp[s]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
