    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef sumOfPowers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # dp[i][j][min_diff] = number of subsequences of length j ending at index i with minimum difference min_diff\n    # This approach is too memory intensive, so we use a different strategy\n    \n    # For each possible minimum difference, count how many subsequences have that as their minimum\n    total = 0\n    \n    # Precompute all differences\n    from itertools import combinations\n    \n    subsequences = list(combinations(range(n), k))\n    \n    for subseq_indices in subsequences:\n        values = [nums[i] for i in subseq_indices]\n        # Find minimum absolute difference\n        min_diff = float('inf')\n        for i in range(len(values)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(values)):\n                min_diff = min(min_diff, abs(values[i] - values[j]))\n        total = (total + min_diff) % MOD\n    \n    return total\n```\n\nWait, let me optimize this with dynamic programming:\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPowers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # dp[i][j] = list of (count, min_power) for subsequences of length j using first i elements\n    # We track minimum power for each subsequence\n    \n    from collections import defaultdict\n    \n    # dp[i][j] = dictionary mapping min_power -> count of subsequences\n    dp = [[defaultdict(int) for _ in range(k + 1)] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    \n    # Base case: empty subsequence\n    dp[0][0][float('inf')] = 1\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(k + 1):\n            # Don't include nums[i-1]\n            for power, count in dp[i-1][j].items():\n                dp[i][j][power] = (dp[i][j][power] + count) % MOD\n            \n            # Include nums[i-1]\n            if j > 0:\n                for power, count in dp[i-1][j-1].items():\n                    if power == float('inf'):\n                        # First element in subsequence\n                        dp[i][j][float('inf')] = (dp[i][j][float('inf')] + count) % MOD\n                    else:\n                        # Not first element, calculate new minimum power\n                        new_power = min(power, abs(nums[i-1] - nums[i-2]))\n                        # Need to track properly based on actual subsequence\n    \n    # Simpler approach: enumerate all subsequences and calculate\n    from itertools import combinations\n    \n    total = 0\n    for indices in combinations(range(n), k):\n        min_diff = float('inf')\n        for i in range(len(indices)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(indices)):\n                min_diff = min(min_diff, abs(nums[indices[i]] - nums[indices[j]]))\n        total = (total + min_diff) % MOD\n    \n    return total\n```",
